# .github-guard — declarations github-guard reads (see the github-guard skill:
# https://github.com/antimatter-studios/agent-skills/blob/main/.claude/skills/github-guard/README.md).
# git-config format: check with `git config -f .github-guard --list`, and
# double-quote any value containing # or ;.

# Required status checks for main. A declaration beats discovery exactly.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS WHEN THE LIST IS ALREADY RIGHT.
#
# On 2026-09-17 branch protection required exactly these five names and
# `ci.yml` produced exactly these five, all green — so this file changes
# nothing today. It is written anyway, because the list it pins lived only
# in the GitHub settings page: a text box with no reviewer, no history in
# this repository, and no relationship to the workflow beside it. Nothing
# there fails when the two drift apart. Add a matrix leg, rename a job, or
# split `test` in two, and the new check reports on every pull request while
# the gate keeps asking for the old set — green, and gating less than it did
# the day before. That is the "fake coverage" audit of
# antimatter-studios/diskjockey#197, sections 1 and 7, and the sibling
# `rust-img-qcow2` is what it looks like when it has already happened: a
# sixth check, `test / i686-unknown-linux-gnu`, produced on every pull
# request and required by nothing.
#
# Discovery is not the answer either. github-guard's fallback requires
# checks BY JOB NAME, which is a guess at the PR gate and drifts the same
# way from the other side: a renamed job, an added workflow, or any future
# check reporting under the github-actions app changes what is required
# without anyone deciding to. Pinning it costs nothing and makes the intent
# reviewable in a pull request, which is the whole point.
#
# WHAT EACH NAME IS AND WHY IT GATES.
#
# All five run on `pull_request` (`ci.yml` triggers on `push` to main and on
# `pull_request` with no branch filter), so all five can gate one. Nothing
# from `release.yml` is declared: it triggers on a `v*.*.*` tag, after the
# merge it would have gated, and a required check that never reports on a
# pull request blocks the repository permanently.
#
#   test / <os>      — the three matrix legs of the `test` job: build,
#                      `cargo test --locked --all-targets`, clippy. The
#                      spelling is GitHub's for a matrix job, `name` then
#                      ` / ` then the matrix value, spaces around the slash
#                      included; it is the check-run name reported on
#                      2847b3a and it is what must be written here.
#                      Declaring all three rather than ubuntu alone is
#                      deliberate: this crate reads and writes a
#                      byte-exact on-disk format, and the Windows leg
#                      already executes one test fewer than the other two.
#   qemu-validation  — cross-validates our reader and writer against
#                      `qemu-img`. It is the only job that runs the
#                      `qemu-validation` feature at all: the `test` legs
#                      build `tests/qemu_validation.rs` with the feature
#                      off, where it compiles to an empty shell and runs 0
#                      tests. Drop this from the required list and the only
#                      check that compares us to another implementation
#                      stops being a gate.
#   fmt              — `cargo fmt --check`.
#
# Names must match the check-run names GitHub reports, including the
# "test / <os>" job-matrix spelling.
[checks]
	required = fmt
	required = qemu-validation
	required = test / ubuntu-latest
	required = test / macos-latest
	required = test / windows-latest
